HOW TO RUN THIS DISK====================This disk is NOT a boot disk, and os9exec does not boot anything.  There isno kernel here and no OS9Boot file.  os9exec is the kernel: you point it at adisk with OS9DISK and name a program to run, and that disk is what OS-9 thensees as /dd -- its root and its home.  There is no established name for thatrole, which is why people reach for "boot disk"; this disk cannot bootanything.Of the 444 programs in CMDS and CMDS/GAMES, 343 need nothing but this disk.The other 92 are starred in DOC/INDEX and want Microware's cio (seeDOC/README-CIO).WHERE THE PROGRAMS THINK THEY LIVE----------------------------------94 of them carry a hardcoded /h0 path.  That is not damage: they were builtby people whose freeware lived on /h0.69 of the 94 want just one file, /h0/sys/termcap -- and every one of thosereads the TERMCAP environment variable first.  SYS/login sets it to thisdisk's own copy, so those 69 work wherever the disk is mounted, with no /h0in sight.  That was measured, program by program, with no exceptions.What is left is 37 programs wanting data of their own under /h0, like/h0/games/hack/playground; most of the 37 are the gcc passes and thelinker.  Going the other way, 20 programs read their data from /dd --advent wants /dd/GAMES/adv/glorkz, fortune /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat,nroff /dd/LIB/tmac.*.No single arrangement satisfies both sets, which is what the three below arereally about.  DOC/DEPENDS lists every path each program opens.------------------------------------------------------------------1. YOU ALREADY RUN OS-9  --  recommended------------------------------------------------------------------Keep your own system as /dd and mount this collection as /h0:    OS9DISK=<your own disk>  OS9H0=<this image, named h0>  os9exec shellThen add this disk's commands to your path, in your own startup:    setenv PATH /dd/CMDS:/h0/CMDS:/h0/CMDS/GAMESPATH works in the OS-9 shell -- a bare command name is found along it, notonly in the execution directory.  (Verified: with /h0/CMDS on PATH a bare`rndname' runs; with it removed the same command gives E_PNNF.)This is the best arrangement, and not only for the paths:  - /dd stays yours -- your CMDS, your LIB, your startup, your cio.  - Because your cio is on /dd, the STARRED programs work too, so you get    all 427 rather than the 335 that need nothing.  - Every hardcoded /h0 path resolves against this disk, where its data is.What it costs: the 20 programs that read from /dd will look on YOUR disk andnot find their files.  Copy the data they want onto your own /dd, or runthose few with this image as /dd instead (arrangement 2).For a shell with this disk's commands, TERMCAP and a working cd already setup, hand bash the login script -- it works out where the disk is mountedfrom the path you give it:    os9exec -r bash /h0/SYS/loginThe image must be a file NAMED h0: os9exec resolves an image by filename, soOS9H0 pointing at a file called anything else fails with error 214.Two things to know:  - If /h0 is already YOUR disk, keep it that way and see arrangement 3.  - The image must stay writable.  hack, larn and cribbage write into    /h0/games/*/playground and criblog.------------------------------------------------------------------2. YOU HAVE NO OS-9 OF YOUR OWN------------------------------------------------------------------Run this disk by itself.  It carries public-domain shells, so no Microwaremodule is needed anywhere:    OS9DISK=<this image>  os9exec -r bash /dd/SYS/loginThat trailing /dd/SYS/login matters.  Started bare, bash has no PATH, noHOME and no TERM, so it finds no command; SYS/login exports those and handsover to an interactive shell.  Setting HOME is also what makes bash read/dd/.bashrc, where cd, pwd and the aliases are defined.This gets you the 335 unstarred programs, and -- because SYS/login setsTERMCAP -- the 69 that would otherwise want /h0/sys/termcap.  Only the 37with other /h0 data are out of reach, and they are mostly compiler passes.If you want those 37 as well, give os9exec the image under a second name:    ln -s osk-freeware.dd h0    OS9DISK=<...>/osk-freeware.dd  OS9H0=<...>/h0  os9exec -r bash /dd/SYS/loginos9exec will not mount one path as two devices, so it needs two names; asymlink is enough and costs nothing.  Understand what it is, though: ONEimage behind two device names, each with its own sector cache.  Reading isfine.  Writing through both at once is not, so do not count on a scorewritten via /h0 being visible via /dd in the same session.Caveats when running the disk alone:  - PATH search works from the IMAGE and not from a host directory.  Run    the .dd and a bare `cookie' is found; point OS9DISK at an unpacked    directory instead and the same command says "command not found" with    PATH set and the program sitting there.  Reading .bashrc fails the same    way, with E$Unit.  Full paths always work.  - `sh', which the startup script names as its shell, starts but cannot    fork ANY external command.  It sees the file, agrees it is executable,    and the fork still fails.  Use bash.  - `ksh' is the nicest of the three but wants cio loaded before it starts,    which needs a shell first -- so it cannot be the first shell.------------------------------------------------------------------3. TAKE ONLY WHAT YOU WANT------------------------------------------------------------------Nobody needs SNOBOL, FORTRAN and XLISP at once.  To move a few programs ontoyour own disk and keep your own /h0:  - Copy this disk's GAMES/ to your own disk, so /h0/games/... resolves    against yours.  That is what the game data paths expect.  - Read DOC/DEPENDS before copying any single program.  It lists, per    program, every absolute path that program carries -- generated by    scanning the binaries.  110 of them need something besides their own    binary: a dictionary, a skeleton file, a scratch directory.  Copying    just the command often is not enough.  - Your own /h0/sys/termcap is then the one that gets used, which is    probably better tuned for your terminal than ours.  Or set TERMCAP and    the question does not arise.------------------------------------------------------------------THE SHELL YOU GET------------------------------------------------------------------bash 1.12 with /dd/.bashrc read, which matters more than it sounds.ALWAYS HAND BASH THE LOGIN SCRIPT.  Started bare --`os9exec /h0/CMDS/bash' -- you get a shell with no PATH, so `ls' is notfound; no HOME, so .bashrc is never read and bash complains about/.bashrc; and a `cd' that ABORTS THE SHELL on first use.  Everythingbelow depends on SYS/login having run.With no OS9DISK set, /dd is a host directory rather than an RBF disk, andbash prints `shell-init: getwd: cannot access parent directories' once atstartup.  It is harmless and there is nothing to fix: SYS/login spends thesame failure deliberately, in a script where it cannot be fatal, which isexactly what leaves cd working afterwards.What you get:  - cd and pwd WORK, but only because .bashrc replaces them.  bash's own    getwd() walks `..' upward looking for a directory that is its own    parent.  OS-9 has one root per DEVICE and nothing above them, so that    walk never ends: plain `pwd' HANGS THE SHELL, and where /dd is not an    RBF device plain `cd' aborts it with a bus error.  If a terminal here    has ever had to be closed rather than exited, this is the likeliest    reason.  The replacements track the path by string instead.  - ls, cat, less, wc, basename and dirname all run with no cio.  dir,    edir and l are the Microware-style listers and are starred; ls is the    one to use.  - vi_nocio is the editor that needs nothing -- PVic, cio-free, and it    drives a vt100 without any termcap file at all.  Plain `vi' is the EFFO    build and vi_cio is starred.  - `exit' leaves.  So does `quit' in most of the games; read DOC/INDEX    before starting one you cannot get out of.DOC/INDEX says what each program is; DOC/ORIGINS says where it came from;SOURCES.txt records the terms it arrived under.